  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, federal contract obligations in Oklahoma total $3.6B, spread across 15,048 awards with an average award size of $241K. Obligations are down 46.8% year over year. This fiscal-year-to-date total follows a multi-year sequence of obligations: FY21 $4.5B, FY22 $5B, FY23 $4.9B, FY24 $5.5B, and FY25 $6.8B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer so far in FY2026, with $2.4B in obligations. Other sizable buying agencies include the Department of Transportation at $490M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $218M. Industry spend is concentrated in aerospace and engineering related NAICS, led by Aircraft Manufacturing at $636M and Engineering Services at $342M. THE BOEING COMPANY is the top vendor by obligations at $770M, followed by MCKESSON CORPORATION at $149M and another entry for THE BOEING COMPANY at $139M. These figures are contract obligations and reflect fiscal year to date totals that are partial for the current year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big is Oklahoma's federal market right now?",
        "body": "Oklahoma's federal market stands at $3.6B in obligations for FY2026 to date. Activity is spread across 15,048 awards with an average award size of $241K, and overall obligations are down 46.8% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who and what are driving the spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of buying at $2.4B, followed by the Department of Transportation at $490M and HHS at $218M. Industry concentrations include Aircraft Manufacturing at $636M and Engineering Services at $342M, and THE BOEING COMPANY is the largest contractor at $770M."
      }
    ]
